---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/CaseStudySection.astro";

const threads = [
  {
    key: "arrival",
    label: "Getting in",
    owned: "Built event pages, Stripe ticketing, QR check-in, and staff lookup tools, with one-time email links and passkeys for attendee access.",
    enables: "Guests have an admission path; staff can distinguish a valid ticket from one already used."
  },
  {
    key: "participation",
    label: "Joining in",
    owned: "Built attendee tools and event scoring, and connected games to the event platform.",
    enables: "A match can contribute to the wider event without making the game depend on its points system."
  },
  {
    key: "followup",
    label: "Following up",
    owned: "Built event communications and connected the site to galleries and Pitch Night.",
    enables: "The event has a place for information before the night and things people want to return to afterwards."
  }
];
const mechanisms = [
  {
    thread: "arrival",
    layer: "Make sign-in easy without outsourcing the account model",
    detail: "Guests can use a one-time email link or code, then add a passkey. The app owns its sessions and keeps staff access separately scoped, so a guest login does not become an admin credential."
  },
  {
    thread: "arrival",
    layer: "Fulfil a paid ticket once",
    detail: "Stripe Checkout handles payment. A signed webhook triggers ticket fulfilment, and a Postgres idempotency record prevents a repeated delivery from issuing a second ticket."
  },
  {
    thread: "arrival",
    layer: "Let the database settle admission",
    detail: "Online check-in claims a valid, unused ticket in a Postgres transaction. If two scanners submit the same ticket, only one can claim it. Offline admission still needs reconciliation; it cannot settle a duplicate across disconnected devices."
  },
  {
    thread: "participation",
    layer: "Keep playing separate from points",
    detail: "Games publish their results; the event layer decides eligibility and event points. A points feature should not be required to finish a match."
  }
];
const initialThread = "arrival";
---

<CaseStudyLayout title="Running the night" description="How I build Milk & Henny around the events I host: tickets, arrival, participation, and follow-up." threads={threads} initialThread={initialThread}>
  <p slot="intro">I host events and build the software around them. Milk &amp; Henny connects the practical parts of a night: finding the details, getting a ticket, arriving, joining in, and finding the photos afterwards.</p>
  <dl slot="meta" class="case-meta" aria-label="Case study summary">
    <div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Milk & Henny / Founder &amp; Host / 2025–26</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Focus</dt><dd>Events, passwordless access, ticketing, admission</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Project</dt><dd><a href="https://milkandhenny.com">milkandhenny.com</a></dd></div>
  </dl>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-problem" title="Problem">
    <p>A guest needs to know where to go and what to do next. The person at the door needs a quick answer about admission. As the host, I need to keep those experiences connected without spending the night moving information between separate tools.</p>
  </CaseStudySection>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-contribution" title="My contribution">
    <table class="case-ledger">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">What I built and why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{threads.map((thread) => (
        <tr data-thread={thread.key} data-active={thread.key === initialThread ? true : undefined}>
          <th scope="row">{thread.label}</th><td><p>{thread.owned}</p><p>{thread.enables}</p></td>
        </tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </CaseStudySection>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-decisions" title="Decisions">
    <div class="case-prose">
      <table class="case-ledger">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Choice</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{mechanisms.map((mechanism) => (
          <tr data-thread={mechanism.thread} data-active={mechanism.thread === initialThread ? true : undefined}>
            <th scope="row">{mechanism.layer}</th><td>{mechanism.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}</tbody>
      </table>
      <figure class="case-diagram"><figcaption>A night, from two sides</figcaption><ol>
<li><strong>Before</strong><span>Guests find the event and get tickets.</span><span>Hosts publish details and manage availability.</span></li>
<li><strong>At the door</strong><span>Guests show a ticket.</span><span>Staff scan it or use a lookup path.</span></li>
<li><strong>In the room</strong><span>People play and take part.</span><span>Hosts run activities and manage event points.</span></li>
</ol></figure>
    </div>
  </CaseStudySection>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-result" title="What it enables">
    <div class="case-prose"><p>The platform supports a guest journey and the staff work behind it in one application. I can build around the event itself, rather than treating tickets, activities, and the archive as unrelated sites.</p><p><a href="https://milkandhenny.com/things/pitches">Pitch Night Studio</a> is one example: people can prepare a deck, publish an edition, and present it on a shared screen with phone controls.</p></div>
  </CaseStudySection>

  <CaseStudySection id="case-lesson" title="Lesson">
    <p>Hosting gives the interface a practical test: can someone understand it while a queue is forming or a room is waiting? That makes the recovery path part of the main design. A staff member needs a next step when a scan fails, as much as a green confirmation when it works.</p>
  </CaseStudySection>
</CaseStudyLayout>
